    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>The short answer: count your entry points</h2>
<p>The honest answer to “how many CCTV cameras do I need” starts with your doors, not a number. Burglars and package thieves mostly use doors, gates and ground-floor windows, so the most reliable way to answer “how many security cameras do I need for my house” is to walk around the outside and list every way in. Each door or gate that a person could use without climbing usually deserves a camera. Then add views for the driveway and any valuables stored outside, such as a shed, boat or workshop.</p>
<p>Use the planner below to get a starting number for your property, then read on for zone-by-zone detail.</p>
<!--tool:planner-->
<p>You can also open the <a href="/tools/cctv-camera-planner/">CCTV camera planner</a> on its own page and save a plan to revisit later.</p>

<h2>How many cameras for a home? Typical counts by property</h2>
<p>These ranges are starting points based on the number of entry points and open areas typical of each property type. Your layout may push you up or down.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Property type</th><th>Typical camera count</th><th>Usual zones covered</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Apartment or condo</td><td>1–2</td><td>Front door (inside or doorbell, if allowed), balcony or patio</td></tr>
<tr><td>Townhouse</td><td>2–4</td><td>Front door, back patio or yard gate, garage or parking space</td></tr>
<tr><td>Detached house, suburban lot</td><td>4–6</td><td>Front door, driveway, back door, one or two side gates, backyard</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corner lot or large suburban lot</td><td>6–8</td><td>As above plus a second street frontage and longer fence lines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large lot, acreage, rural</td><td>6–12+</td><td>House zones plus outbuildings, gate, long driveway, equipment areas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Farm or ranch</td><td>8–20+</td><td>Barns, fuel, livestock areas, gates, machinery yards</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>If you’re planning for land rather than a house, see <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-rural-property/">solar cameras for rural property</a> and <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-for-farms/">solar cameras for farms</a>.</p>

<h2>Security camera placement guide: zone-by-zone priorities</h2>
<p>Not every zone is equally important, and this is where most of the answer to how many CCTV cameras do I need comes from. Cover them roughly in this order, and stop when you hit your budget.</p>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/placement-options.webp" alt="Security camera placement options around a house: front door, driveway, side gate and backyard" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Cover entry points first, then the approaches to them.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>1. Front door</h3>
<p>The most-used entry and where package theft happens. Mount a camera or video doorbell at about 1.5–2.5 m so it sees faces rather than the tops of heads. A slightly downward angle that includes the step and the doorstep area captures both faces and packages.</p>
<h3>2. Driveway and parking</h3>
<p>Vehicles, car break-ins and arrivals. A camera on the garage or house corner looking down the driveway catches people approaching the house as well as cars. If you want license plates, plan a separate, narrower view at the point where cars slow down. Our guide to <a href="/guides/solar-camera-driveway-alert-phone/">solar driveway cameras with phone alerts</a> covers angles and alert setup.</p>
<h3>3. Back door and patio</h3>
<p>Back doors are less visible from the street, which makes them attractive to intruders. A camera here is often the second most valuable after the front door.</p>
<h3>4. Side gates and side yards</h3>
<p>Narrow side passages are natural routes to the backyard. A camera on the house wall looking along the passage covers the gate and anyone walking through.</p>
<h3>5. Garage</h3>
<p>Garages hold bikes, tools and often a door into the house. Cover the garage door from outside, and consider one inside if the garage is detached.</p>
<h3>6. Backyard and pool</h3>
<p>Broad open areas suit a wide-angle camera or a pan-tilt camera on a corner. See <a href="/guides/360-degree-solar-camera/">360 degree solar cameras</a> for when a single PTZ can cover a yard.</p>
<h3>7. Outbuildings and gates</h3>
<p>Sheds, workshops, barns and driveway gates are where solar shines, because there’s usually no outlet. Read <a href="/guides/solar-gate-camera/">solar gate cameras</a> for gate-specific advice.</p>
<h3>8. Indoors</h3>
<p>Indoor cameras covering the main hallway or living area catch anyone who gets past exterior cameras. Many owners disable them while home for privacy.</p>
<p>Indoor cameras don’t need solar, and they’re cheap, so one or two often add more value per dollar than a seventh outdoor camera. Place them where an intruder must pass, such as the hallway from the back door to the bedrooms, rather than pointing at a window, where glare and IR reflection ruin the image at night.</p>

<h2>Coverage overlap and blind spots</h2>
<p>A single camera on each wall can still leave gaps. Cameras mounted flat on a wall can’t see the wall itself, so someone hugging the siding can walk below one camera without being recorded clearly. Two habits fix most blind spots:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mount at corners, angled along walls.</strong> A camera at the corner of the house looking along the side wall sees both the wall and the yard beside it.</li>
<li><strong>Overlap fields of view by a few meters.</strong> Where one camera’s view ends, the next should already be seeing that spot. This also gives you two angles on anyone in the overlap.</li>
</ul>
<p>Sketch your house from above, draw each camera’s viewing wedge, and shade what’s left. Anything unshaded near an entry point is a gap worth fixing.</p>
<div class="callout tip"><strong>Detection range is shorter than viewing range</strong><p>A battery or solar camera relies on its PIR motion sensor to wake up, typically effective at about 8–12 m. A camera may <em>see</em> 30 m away but only start recording when someone comes within PIR range. Place cameras so the approach to each door falls inside detection range.</p></div>

<h2>Choosing the right lens for each zone</h2>
<p>Camera count and lens choice are linked. Wide lenses cover more area but show less detail at distance; narrow lenses see further but cover a slice. A rough guide for a 2K–4K camera:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Distance to subject</th><th>Suggested lens / field of view</th><th>Goal</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Front door</td><td>1–4 m</td><td>Wide (110–160°) or doorbell</td><td>Faces and packages</td></tr>
<tr><td>Driveway overview</td><td>5–15 m</td><td>Standard (90–110°)</td><td>See people and vehicles arrive</td></tr>
<tr><td>License plates</td><td>5–10 m at a choke point</td><td>Narrow (30–60°) or zoom</td><td>Readable plates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Side passage</td><td>2–10 m</td><td>Standard to narrow (60–100°)</td><td>Faces walking toward camera</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backyard</td><td>5–20 m</td><td>Wide (110°+) or pan-tilt</td><td>Detect activity anywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td>Long fence line or gate</td><td>10–30 m</td><td>Narrow or telephoto</td><td>Detail at distance</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>To check whether a given lens and resolution will identify a person at your distances, use the <a href="/tools/camera-coverage-calculator/">camera coverage calculator</a>. As a reference point, guidance like the European standard EN 62676-4 uses pixel density targets: roughly 250 pixels per meter to identify a person and about 125 to recognize someone known to you.</p>

<h2>Solar vs wired for each zone</h2>
<p>You don’t have to choose one technology for the whole property. Many homes use wired or plug-in cameras near the house and solar cameras where cable runs are long. The <a href="/compare/solar-vs-wired/">solar vs wired comparison</a> goes deeper; here’s a zone summary:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Usually best</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Front door</td><td>Wired doorbell or solar</td><td>Doorbell wiring often exists; solar works if the porch gets sun</td></tr>
<tr><td>Driveway</td><td>Either</td><td>Wired if near an outlet or soffit; solar if not</td></tr>
<tr><td>Back door, side gates</td><td>Solar or battery</td><td>Avoids drilling and long cable runs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Detached garage, shed, barn</td><td>Solar</td><td>No outlet; trenching cable costs more than the camera</td></tr>
<tr><td>Driveway gate, field</td><td>Solar + 4G or Wi-Fi bridge</td><td>Far from power and often from Wi-Fi</td></tr>
<tr><td>North-facing, heavily shaded walls</td><td>Wired, or solar with remote panel</td><td>Too little direct sun for a camera-mounted panel</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Before committing to solar on a shaded wall, run the <a href="/tools/solar-camera-site-check/">solar camera site check</a> and read <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-direct-sunlight/">do solar cameras need direct sunlight</a>. If you plan several cameras near each other, <a href="/guides/how-many-solar-cameras-can-one-panel-power/">how many solar cameras one panel can power</a> explains sharing panels.</p>

<h2>How much do multiple security cameras cost?</h2>
<p>Ranges below are typical US retail at time of writing (late 2026) for DIY-installed consumer gear and vary widely by brand and features.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Cameras</th><th>Typical hardware cost</th><th>Ongoing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Apartment starter</td><td>1–2 battery or plug-in</td><td>$50–$250</td><td>Optional cloud plan</td></tr>
