<tr><td>Detached home, wired PoE + NVR</td><td>4–8</td><td>$500–$1,500 plus install</td><td>None typically</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rural / acreage, solar + 4G</td><td>6–12</td><td>$1,000–$3,500+</td><td>Data plans per 4G camera</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>For a five-year comparison including electrician costs and subscriptions, try the <a href="/tools/solar-vs-wired-cost-calculator/">solar vs wired cost calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>How many outdoor cameras is too many?</h2>
<p>More cameras isn’t automatically better. Each one adds clips to review, notifications to manage, batteries to monitor and potential privacy friction with neighbors. Signs you’ve overbought:</p>
<ul>
<li>Two cameras show nearly identical views with no added detail.</li>
<li>You’ve turned off notifications because there are too many to read.</li>
<li>Cameras are aimed at the street or neighbors’ property more than your own.</li>
<li>Your Wi-Fi struggles, causing clips to arrive late.</li>
</ul>
<p>A well-placed set of five usually beats a scattered set of ten. Spend the difference on better lenses or a 4G camera for the remote spot that actually matters.</p>

<h2>Do you need a camera on every side of the house?</h2>
<p>Not always. A side with no doors, no gate and no ground-floor windows, bordered by a tall fence or a neighbor’s wall, is low priority. What matters is whether someone could approach an entry point from that side without being seen by another camera. If the only way into that side passage is through a gate already covered by a camera, you can often skip a dedicated camera on the wall.</p>
<p>The exceptions are sides with sliding patio doors, basement windows or easy-to-climb fences. Ground-floor windows hidden from the street are a common entry route, and a single camera angled along that wall covers several windows at once.</p>

<h2>How many cameras for an apartment, condo or rental?</h2>
<p>Apartment owners and renters have fewer zones but more rules. Common setups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>One camera:</strong> an indoor camera facing the front door from inside, or a video doorbell or peephole camera if the building allows it.</li>
<li><strong>Two cameras:</strong> add a camera for a ground-floor patio, balcony or private parking space.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hallways and shared areas are usually controlled by the building, and leases often restrict drilling. Battery and solar cameras on clamp or adhesive mounts avoid most damage; see <a href="/how-to/install-without-drilling/">how to install a camera without drilling</a>.</p>

<h2>Can your network and storage handle multiple cameras?</h2>
<p>Adding cameras adds load. Battery and solar cameras that record only motion events are light on Wi-Fi because they upload short clips, but four to six cameras all streaming live view at once can strain a router, especially if outdoor signal is weak. Wired PoE systems recording 24/7 to a network video recorder (NVR) need hard-drive space instead: continuous 4K recording can use tens of gigabytes per camera per day, so a multi-terabyte drive is typical.</p>
<p>For solar cameras with microSD cards, each camera stores its own footage, so storage scales automatically. If you use 4G cameras at remote spots, each needs its own data allowance; estimate it with the <a href="/tools/4g-data-and-storage-calculator/">4G data and storage calculator</a>.</p>
<div class="keyfacts">
<div><b>4–6</b><span>cameras for a typical detached home</span></div>
<div><b>2.5–3 m</b><span>sweet-spot mounting height</span></div>
<div><b>8–12 m</b><span>typical PIR detection range</span></div>
</div>

<h2>Should you start small and add cameras later?</h2>
<p>Yes, that’s a sensible approach, as long as you choose a system that grows. Starting with the front door and driveway lets you learn how alerts, storage and battery life behave at your property before buying more. Before you buy the first camera, check that the brand’s app handles multiple cameras well, that any hub supports your eventual count, and that cloud plans don’t charge per camera in a way that gets expensive at six. Solar battery cameras are particularly easy to expand because each one is self-contained.</p>

<h2>Common placement mistakes</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Mounting too high</strong><p>Cameras at 4–5 m capture the tops of heads and hats. About 2.5–3 m is a good compromise between tamper resistance and usable faces.</p></li>
<li><strong>Pointing into the sun</strong><p>East- or west-facing cameras can be blinded at sunrise or sunset. Angle them slightly downward and away from the horizon.</p></li>
<li><strong>Ignoring night lighting</strong><p>IR night vision typically reaches about 8–15 m on small cameras. Beyond that, add lighting or expect silhouettes. See <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-at-night/">do solar cameras work at night</a>.</p></li>
<li><strong>Putting the solar panel in shade</strong><p>Eaves, trees and north-facing walls starve panels. A separate panel on a cable often solves this.</p></li>
<li><strong>Relying on one wide camera for everything</strong><p>Wide views are great for context but poor for identification at distance. Pair them with a closer camera at entry points.</p></li>
<li><strong>Forgetting Wi-Fi at the far end of the yard</strong><p>Test signal at each mounting spot before buying. See <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-wifi/">do solar cameras need Wi-Fi</a> for options.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>Privacy and legal considerations</h2>
<p>Generally, recording your own property and areas visible from a public street is lawful in the US, but rules vary by state and locality, and HOAs or leases may add restrictions. Avoid aiming cameras into neighbors’ windows or private yards, and be cautious with audio: many states require consent from all parties for audio recording, which is stricter than video. Renters should read <a href="/guides/rental-property-outdoor-camera-no-subscription/">outdoor cameras for rental properties</a>. For general security tips, the <a href="https://www.ready.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ready.gov</a> home safety resources are a useful government starting point.</p>

<h2>How many CCTV cameras do I need? A sample plan</h2>
<p>For a typical detached suburban house with a two-car driveway, a fenced backyard and one side gate, a balanced plan looks like this:</p>
<ul>
<li>Front door: video doorbell or wide camera (1)</li>
<li>Driveway: corner camera looking down the drive (1)</li>
<li>Side gate: camera on the house wall looking along the passage (1)</li>
<li>Backyard and back door: corner camera covering both, or one each (1–2)</li>
<li>Detached shed: solar camera (1, optional)</li>
</ul>
<p>That’s five or six cameras, matching the typical range. Run your own layout through the <a href="/tools/cctv-camera-planner/">camera planner</a>, then size solar panels with the <a href="/tools/solar-camera-battery-calculator/">battery calculator</a>. When you’re ready to choose hardware, our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> guide and <a href="/solar-camera-installation/">installation guide</a> pick up from here.</p>
